   Cochlear Implant Team},
Abstract = {The aim of this study was a retrospective review of all of our patients
   requiring reimplantation. Since 1990, 305 cochlear implants (CI) have
   been implanted in adults and children; 12 adults (8.5\%) and 14 children
   (8.5\%) have been reimplanted. The main reason has been due to internal
   device failure and identifiable trauma (6.5\%). Other causes were due to
   medical problems (0.6\%) and upgrade from earlier implant systems
   (1.3\%). In most cases, implants suddenly failed; in others, there was
   progressive failure. In children, the problems were mainly identified by
   either parent or child. Reimplantation was carried out successfully in
   all cases who elected for a new Cl and without complication. The adult
   patients who were surgically upgraded from single channel devices
   received significant benefit. Children showed continuous development of
   speech perception. Whilst the need for cochlear reimplantation is a
   major complication, the additional cost is only a small proportion of
   the total cost. One parent elected not to have further surgery and a
   second child became a nonuser. The cost to health care is significant if
   a patient does not use their device. (c) 2004 Elsevier B.V. All rights
   reserved.},
