   13-15, 1997},
Abstract = {Skilled actions are characterised by a smooth coupling of perception and
   action, resulting in the right movements and coordination mode at the
   right moment. The focus of this paper is on the acquisition of skill and
   on the role of intentions in this process. As a theoretical framework
   the Psychological Action Theory is shortly outlined. It ir claimed that
   during learning and skill acquisition new intentions are formed on the
   basis of the perceived and appraised situation. Means, for example motor
   acts, which are likely to result in the expected outcome: are linked Li
   with these intentions. An idiographic and hermeneutic analysis is
   propose ci as the key for understanding intentional actions and the
   development of intentions-means relations. Results from two learning
   experiments show how intention-means-hierarchies evolve ton the basis of
   the assessment of the situation and experiences and how new intentions
   and means are formed. Conclusions are drawn with respect to the
   underlying internal structure of skills and the possible implications
   for teaching and instructing in sport settings. The need for an
   integrated psychological understanding of skill acquisition is
   emphasised.},
