   Chartered Inst Bldg (CIOB); Council Sustainable Dev; Emerald; Hong Kong
   Inst Architects; Hong Kong Institut Engineers; Hong Kong Inst Surveyors;
   HKSTS; IUE; JSCE; RICS; Urban Planning Soc China; China Civil Engn Soc;
   Hong Kong Inst Util Specialists},
Abstract = {Ageing of building stock is emerging. It is predicted that the ageing
   trend will persist and spread out from more developed regions. We cannot
   tell exactly the fate of the aged stock is - as we are not prophets, but
   we are pretty sure that building maintenance will be more frequent. In
   light of the ageing trend, design, construct and manage for
   maintainability is a feasible way out to contend with the older building
   stock and the growing maintenance demand. The aim of this paper
   therefore sets to review the latest position in building maintainability
   researches and develop a theoretical framework of managing for building
   maintainability that incorporates the idea of sustainable development.
   These are done by a literature review in maintainability engineering,
   building maintenance and building maintainability. The idea of building
   maintainability is re-examined. A theoretical framework of building
   maintainability embracing managerial elements is developed. Meanwhile,
   an overview of ongoing researches is provided. At present, there are
   only scattered studies in the subject. They largely centred on design
   and technological aspects and do not adequately consider sustainable
   development. This study fills the gap and looks for means to create a
   more maintainable and thus sustainable built-environment to cope with
   the problem of building stock ageing.},
