Year = {2014},
Pages = {25-29},
Note = {16th European Conference on Developmental Psychology, Lausanne,
   SWITZERLAND, SEP 03-07, 2013},
Abstract = {Although the importance of the development of phonological awareness in
   children with hearing impairment has been highlighted in recent years,
   currently in Spain there are no specific instruments for assessing
   phonological awareness for this population. Therefore, the main goal of
   this study was to evaluate phonological awareness in 17 children with
   hearing impairment who used some technical support (cochlear implant and
   / or headset) through Oral Language Assessment to Written Language test
   (LolEva-for its acronym in Spanish), which had never been applied to
   children with these characteristics.
   It was possible to test the relationship between phonological awareness
   and variables such as chronological age, the kind of audiological help,
   educational level and the elapsed time since the onset of auditory
   perception. The results showed statistical relationships between these
   variables and most phonological awareness levels, except for the rhyme.
   The main conclusion was that the test LolEva is useful in the assessment
   of phonological awareness of children with hearing impairment,
   demonstrating the difficulty presented in recognition of the rhyme, and
   in the resolution of tasks requiring the memorization of sounds while
   performing another action (eg.: addition and omission of syllables and
   phonemes).},
