Pages = {415-422},
Note = {8th International Congress on Obesity, PARIS, FRANCE, AUG 29-SEP 03,
   1998},
Organization = {Int Assoc Study Obesity},
Abstract = {In summary, tissue specific alterations in the expression and regulation
   of lipoprotein lipase (LPL) influence triglyceride-rich lipoprotein
   triglyceride fatty acid trafficking in a way that preserves body
   composition. The role of this tissue-specific expression in predicting
   changes in body composition in weight stable subjects remains
   inadequately examined. However, in weight-reduced subjects, increases in
   adipose tissue LPL and/or the responsiveness of the enzyme to meals or
   insulin, and decreases in the enzyme in skeletal muscle favor lipid fuel
   storage rather than oxidation. Transgenic mice which overexpress LPL in
   skeletal muscle are either leaner or resistant to high fat
   feeding-induced obesity. This effect appears to be gender-specific, i.e.
   in male but not female mice. Finally, the importance of insulin action
   to adipose tissue mass preservation is under study. In general,
   increases in insulin sensitivity appear to be associated with adipose
   tissue mass gain whereas insulin resistance may be an adaptation for
   body fat maintenance. From preliminary studies in pubertal children,
   this may be adipose tissue region dependent. An important challenge for
   the future is to understand how insulin action in the periphery relates
   to the perception of energy balance by the brain.},
