Note = {20th Regular Meeting of the Barany-Society, WURZBURG, GERMANY, SEP
   12-15, 1998},
Organization = {Barany Soc},
Abstract = {The susceptibility to motion sickness and the perception of self-motion
   are known to decrease during Coriolis cross-coupling stimulation under
   microgravity. However, eye movements under microgravity have not been
   fully described. The purpose of this study was to evaluate the eye
   movements evoked by Coriolis ;stimulation under microgravity using
   computerized eye movement analysis. Two common marmosets (Callithrix
   jacchus) were given Coriolis stimulation (180 degrees/second chair
   rotation and 45 degrees lateral body tilt) during microgravity phases of
   parabolic night and under 1 G condition. Eye movements that were
   recorded on videotapes using an infrared CCD camera were quantitatively
   evaluated by computerized analysis. Under microgravity, the nystagmus
   was oblique and regular whereas it changed gradually from oblique to
   vertical or horizontal under. I G. Under microgravity, the rotation axis
   of the eyeball kept tilting to almost 67.5 degrees loft of vertical.
   Under lc, the rotation asis once tilted to almost 67.5 degrees left,
   then gradually tilted to 90 degrees left or returned to the vertical
   position. This suggests that the rotation axis of the eyeball is
   determined by the theoretical angular velocity vector generated by
   Coriolis stimulation and is affected by gl gravioinertial force level.},
