   Teknologi Malaysia, Inst Sultan Iskandar},
Abstract = {The development of waterfront land has been recognized internationally.
   In Malaysia, upgrading technologies coupled with urbanization and urban
   population growth has transformed use of land along many of the
   waterfront areas. In many waterfront areas, property developers have
   taken advantage by incorporated the water amenities as a ``selling
   point{''} of the development. However, only few numbers of waterfront
   development projects continue to remain profitable while the rest were
   not. The objective of this paper is to identify the current practice of
   waterfront development in the State of Johor, and any subsequent
   barriers to development. The qualitative research strategy has been
   chosen, and in-depth interviews approach with property development
   companies within case study area were undertaken in order to answer
   research objective. As a results, all respondents participated in this
   study were involved in residential waterfront development project. The
   findings also identified Six (6) barriers while developing waterfront
   development projects such as feasible location; bureaucracy in planning
   approval, limited skill workers, cost of development, water polluted and
   perception from community. The findings were then used to improve
   waterfront development practice in Malaysia generally and the State of
   Johor respectively.},
