@inproceedings{ WOS:000181195800002,
Author = {Cox, T and Rial-Gonzalez, E},
Editor = {Rantanen, J and Lehtinen, S},
Title = {Risk management, psychosocial hazards and work stress},
Booktitle = {PROCEEDINGS OF THE INTERNATIONAL SYMPOSIUM PSYCHOLOGICAL STRESS AT WORK},
Series = {PEOPLE AND WORK: RESEARCH REPORTS},
Year = {2000},
Volume = {32},
Pages = {10-20},
Note = {International Symposium on Psychological Stress at Work, ESPOO, FINLAND,
   JUN 07-09, 1999},
Organization = {WHO; Finnish Inst Occupat Hlth; Finnish Minist Social Affairs \& Hlth},
Abstract = {This paper provides a brief overview of the development of a risk
   management approach to work stress. This development project was
   initiated in the early 1990s with the publication of ``Stress Research
   and Stress Management: Putting Theory to Work{''} (1) and has been
   funded largely by the Health \& Safety Executive but with additional
   support from other bodies such as the British Medical Association. The
   Health \& Safety Executive is the government agency responsible within
   Great Britain for promoting, monitoring and enforcing good practice in
   relation to occupational safety and health.
   The starting point for the development of the risk management approach
   lies in the changing nature of work and of work problems and
   work-related ill health. Examples can be taken from the recent European
   Surveys of Working Conditions (2, 3) and from aspects of the UK Labour
   Force Surveys. The interpretative framework implicit in the analysis of
   the data from the Labour Force Survey, relevant to the question of work
   stress, is clearly that of the traditional health and safety equation of
   HAZARD > HARM > RISK. However, there is the additional suggestion that
   work stress might be a mediating factor in the relationship between
   hazard exposure and subsequent harm. If it is possible to treat work
   stress as a contemporary health and safety issue, then it makes sense to
   attempt to deal with that issue in the same way as most other health and
   safety issues are currently dealt with - through the application of the
   risk management paradigm. This paradigm is essentially an attempt at
   systematic and logical problem solving. This paper describes the way in
   which occupational health psychologists at the Institute of Work, Health
   \& Organisations in Nottingham have developed this paradigm in terms of
   four sequential stages: risk analysis and assessment, translation,
   interventions for risk reduction, and evaluation and organizational
   learning. It is critically important to begin the risk management
   process with a ``good enough{''} assessment of the risk associated with
   the psychosocial hazards in relation to the experience of stress and
   their effects on health, and health-related and organizational
   behaviours.
   The adaptation of traditional approaches to risk assessment to the
   question of psychosocial hazards and work stress cannot be ``rocket
   science{''}. All that is required, at this stage, is a systematic
   attempt to collect data within the explicit framework of a risk
   assessment paradigm, drawing not only on the logic of such an approach
   and on good practice in applied science but also on existing legal
   requirements. This type of thinking finds both favour and encouragement
   in the United Kingdom's and the European Union's legislation and
   guidance on health and safety at work. In the United Kingdom, employers
   have been further encouraged to take this issue seriously by the
   development of relevant case law, such as Walker vs Northumberland
   County Council (4), and by an increasing number of employee liability
   claims.
   The effective application of a risk assessment for psychosocial hazards
   and work stress, and of the overall risk management paradigm, requires
   attention to be paid to a number of important process issues. Particular
   emphasis has been placed by the Nottingham team on the status of workers
   and their managers as ``experts{''} in relation to their own jobs, and
   the collection of data from them may be cast as an exercise in knowledge
   elicitation and modelling. For this to work well, both have to be
   involved - participate- in both the risk assessment and risk reduction
   phases of the risk management project. To do so effectively they have to
   be educated in the project and their expectations suitably shaped.
   Finally, some of the issues raised by the risk management approach in
   relation to applied science have to be considered. The comparison of the
   risk management approach to more traditional research paradigms in work
   stress is another story but one well worth contemplating. The
   differences are important and often psychologists fail to understand
   them.},
