@inproceedings{ WOS:000179444800144,
Author = {Sastrosasmito, S},
Editor = {Ural, O and Abrantes, V and Tadeu, A},
Title = {The practice of self-help housing concepts in Indonesia},
Booktitle = {XXX IAHS WORLD CONGRESS ON HOUSING, HOUSING CONSTRUCTION: AN
   INTERDISCIPLINARY TASK, VOLS 1-3},
Year = {2002},
Pages = {1171-1179},
Note = {30th IAHS World Congress on Housing, UNIV COIMBRA, COIMBRA, PORTUGAL,
   SEP 09-13, 2002},
Organization = {Int Assoc Housing Sci},
Abstract = {This study has sought and compiled evidence of the present practice of
   self-help housing of three types of settlements in three Indonesian
   cities. The three types of settlements are: (1) Perumnas (formal
   self-help housing), (2) KIP (Kampung Improvement Program-quasi informal
   self-help settlements), and (3) Kampung (informal self-help
   settlements). The three cities that had been choosen as sample of the
   survey are: (1) Jakarta (big city), (2) Yogyakarta (medium city, and (3)
   Jember (small city). The measurement indicators that are utilized in
   assessing the magnitude of self-help housing practices in Perumnas, KIP,
   and kampung are: (1) labour input for construction, (2) construction
   skills, (3) free time availability, (4) financial sources for
   construction, (5) building materials, (6) design, (7) management, (8)
   participation, and (9) maintenance.
   On the basis of survey findings from three types of settlements of three
   cities, the research concludes that, financially, people have their own
   capability to finance their house construction or expansion. The
   findings on self-design, management, participation, and maintenance also
   provide support to an optimistic outlook and positive direction toward
   the adoption of self-help housing concept in the future. On the
   self-design, it was found that the majority of people in the three types
   of settlements of the three cities have done the design of their house
   by them. Regarding management of house construction, the survey revealed
   that all the respondents managed their housing construction by
   themselves. With regard to participation, it was found that
   participation positively influences the rate of physical developments:
   the higher the level of sense of belonging towards the environment, the
   higher was the infrastructure conditions of the settlements.
   However, regarding labor inputs for construction or expansion the survey
   revealed that hired labor dominated the construction or development
   process of house. Almost all people in the three types of settlements of
   the three cities have no free time available for doing
   self-construction. Regarding building materials, the survey findings
   showed that about 86 per cent of houses in Perumnas and 66 and 67 per
   cent respectively of KIP and kampung, use building materials produced
   nationally (often by the large industrial complexes of the formal
   sector) and internationally (imported).
   Regarding the context of cities and types of settlements,
   `self-production' of building materials is usually found in small cities
   and in kampung or in informal self-help settlements, particularly in the
   periphery of city it is very rare to be found in medium and big cities,
   nor in KIP and Perumnas, Self-built and `self-finance' are usually found
   in medium and small cities, and in KIP and kampung. But, they are very
   rare to be found in big cities and Perumnas. In big cities and Perumnas,
   the self-help housing is found to correspond to the concept Of self-help
   in the sense of `self-idea', `self-design', `self-management',
   `self-maintenance' and `self-use'.},
