Pages = {59-65},
Note = {22nd Congress of the International Ergonomics Association-IEA, SOUTH
   KOREA, AUG 25-29, 2024},
Abstract = {yThe objective of this study was to analyze the impact of COVID-19 on
   the health and work conditions of people with sickle cell disease (SCD).
   This is a qualitative, exploratory study, covering workers with SCD and
   users of basic health units in Reconcavo da Bahia-Brazil. The interviews
   were transcribed and transformed into textual corpus for analysis using
   the IRAMUTEQ Software (word cloud and similarity analysis). The data
   were subjected to Bardin's content analysis following the following
   phases: Organization of analysis, coordination, categorization,
   treatment of results, inference and interpretation of results. A strong
   relationship was observed between the fear of contamination and the care
   strategies adopted, taking into account that people with SCD can develop
   severe progression of the disease. The results of the similarity
   analysis showed that the experiences of illness, perceptions, feelings
   and survival strategies during the COVID-19 pandemic compromised quality
   of life and health. The ability to work was affected and there were
   difficulties in the work routine due to the fear of complications
   related to contamination by the virus. Inequalities and barriers to the
   working life of people with SCD were highlighted, as well as the need
   for care, control and balance strategies to maintain quality of life and
   productive capacity.},
