Note = {Joint Conference of 5th International Conference on Sustainable
   Architecture and Urban Design (ICWSAUD) / 5th International Conference
   on Engineering, Innovation, and Technology (ICEIT), Univ Sains Malaysia,
   Sch Housing Bldg \& Planning, ELECTR NETWORK, SEP 22-23, 2020},
Abstract = {This study analyses the place-making of Farlim, a Garden City, in
   Penang, focusing on two of the five urban design elements according to
   Lynch (1960), i.e. path and edge. Farlim is the first largest
   private-developed township in Penang comprising a mixed range of housing
   development as well as some supporting components such as commercial,
   business, healthcare, and educational institutions. The urban layout of
   this garden city is a gridiron plan adapted to the natural contour of
   the site in an irregular layout. To carry out the survey and analysis of
   the elements that compose the mental image of this site, this study uses
   qualitative methods including site visits, online research, and
   interviews. This study shows that the path is the most dominant urban
   design element at this site, ensuring smooth circulation and efficient
   connectivity to cater to the high traffic flow from neighbouring areas.
   The identified strong edge is formed by Bukit Romania, which created a
   clear boundary between the site and the undeveloped hill land. These two
   important urban design elements have successfully created a significant
   visual perception in Farlim, Penang.
