Organization = {Tokyo Inst Psychiat},
Abstract = {Background. The past 5 years has seen an increase in genetic testing for
   familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP), colon cancer and breast cancer.
   With the cloning of the breast cancer (BRCA1 and BRCA2) genes, women are
   requesting genetic testing and many are inappropriate candidates.
   Methods. We queried 391 women (with strong family histories of breast
   cancer) as to their knowledge of breast cancer and genetic testing, as
   well as their interest in and potential decision-making about testing,
   and compared them with 382 age matched controls, from the same
   geographical areas, with no family history of breast cancer in a first
   degree relative.
   Results. The mean age was 44 with a range from 20 to 76 years, primarily
   white (97\%), married (78\%), college educated (59\%), and from cities
   with populations less than 150,000 (68\%). A multiple regression
   analysis revealed that the best predictors of willingness to undergo
   genetic testing were: a greater perception of being a gene mutation
   carrier, fewer negative and more positive aspects to testing, being at a
   higher risk, and a greater breast cancer anxiety (30\% of variance).
   Conclusions. Taking into account the ethical issues involved in testing,
   improved education and access to genetic counselling are essential in
   order to help all women make appropriate decisions about genetic
   testing.},
